        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "93c58ca2-6c05-32b0-b26e-95e4ab161f58",
                "word": "bardcore",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bc7d7741-c4b6-3d39-997a-48f5d8d63eee",
                        "entry_id": "93c58ca2-6c05-32b0-b26e-95e4ab161f58",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Forme de pop de style médiéval."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1dac61ea-1e43-4d13-bf2b-ca9c120723d4",
                                "sense_id": "bc7d7741-c4b6-3d39-997a-48f5d8d63eee",
                                "text": "C’est un hommage venu tout droit du fond des âges. « One more Time », du regretté groupe Daft Punk – récemment séparé –, s’accorde désormais aux sons des cornemuses dans le style entraînant des ritournelles du Moyen Âge. Faites place, gentes dames et fiers damoiseaux, à une des plus originales trouvailles d’Internet, le bien nommé bardcore."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "10ec40a9-3c7c-4069-be44-0b8e272997e1",
                        "source_entry_id": "93c58ca2-6c05-32b0-b26e-95e4ab161f58",
                        "source_sense_id": "bc7d7741-c4b6-3d39-997a-48f5d8d63eee",
                        "target_lang_word": "bardcore",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "bardcore",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5fdf39f1-4e77-33ca-b8ad-3626cc24f92a",
                                        "entry_id": "4bb461d2-f4d8-36cb-b2ac-93c7f2131555",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A genre of music in which modern songs are covered in a medieval or ancient style, often involving the lyrics being adapted or translated into historically accurate language."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "21ea7b54-7868-49dd-9626-052c6a674ef5",
                                                "sense_id": "5fdf39f1-4e77-33ca-b8ad-3626cc24f92a",
                                                "text": "There's a Bardcore cover for every occasion: The Weekend's \"Blinding Lights\" for the after-banquet, Sia's \"Chandelier\" for when thine eyes doth begin to weep, Linkin Park's \"Numb\" for when the mood continues to spiral, and a cover of \"I'm Blue (da ba dee)\" by Eiffel 65 for those yearning for a simpler time."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d947833c-b938-4e4a-a258-5e24a521d376",
                                                "sense_id": "5fdf39f1-4e77-33ca-b8ad-3626cc24f92a",
                                                "text": "Sure, bardcore's usage of modern melodies may not align with what scholars know about medieval melodies (written in an entirely different mode) and the insertion of faux-medieval phrases like taking out all the \"you\"s and using \"thou\" alongside contemporary lyrics may not be entirely accurate. But bardcore's reinvention of medieval music isn't all that different from what's framed as historically accurate medieval music today."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6c3566f7-3a32-4038-a0ed-6019bb018700",
                                                "sense_id": "5fdf39f1-4e77-33ca-b8ad-3626cc24f92a",
                                                "text": "The instruments he uses for the bardcore covers are downloaded from virtual sound libraries - a variety of lutes and harps, as well as the crumhorn, a curved wind instrument, and the psaltery, a kind of plucked zither."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
